    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3f3d93c1-9638-5bf5-9db6-c30441af65c3",
      ":question/problem": "$\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
